Load decks of sp_mos6 against ngspice-45, written by ../load.py mos6.
Builds on PATH: ngspice-45 jfngv2gd3zvi5cmr9vrqfhnziipqd09n (source hfvpfb0bgw9819wkb7kzpdaasbd0hd5r, devices/mos6),
VACASK dhxz6l3iac3r2b8n91kcz04a0j3hs4s6 (devices/spice/mos6.va, ngspice-47-159 vadng-pre-48).
Run: `nix develop .#testing -c tests/fixtures/families/load_run.sh mos6`. i(vd) in A, |i(vd)| at 1 GHz for acg/acd.
nom is 27 C, m=1, the control of off (80 C, m=3); both give w, l, ad, as, pd, ps, a bulk at -0.5 V, junctions (cj, cjsw,
js) and Meyer capacitances (tox, cgso, cgdo, cgbo). Regions: cut vgs=0.3 vds=2, lin vgs=3 vds=0.2, sat vgs=1.5 vds=3.
The oracle of the AC readings (fd.py, last lines): each nominal region's operating point moved 1e-5 V on the gate
(base) and the drain (collector), whose central differences are gm and gds of the DC law, against |i(vd)| at 1 kHz.
It holds where the conductance is well above the capacitive current at 1 kHz and the differences' resolution (ngspice
prints 12 digits: about 5e-11 S): not in cutoff, whose 1 kHz reading is the capacitances'.
Result: the DC law agrees in every region (1e-5 at most, a cutoff current). ngspice-45 has no AC for level 6:
its device table gives no AC load (devices/mos6/mos6init.c:43, `.DEVacLoad = NULL`), and every AC deck aborts with
"doAnalyses: matrix is singular", so its column is empty; VACASK's AC readings are its central differences (2e-10).
Class: an analysis one simulator lacks for a family (no AC, noise or other small-signal analysis on level 6).

